---
description: Compute/derive/aggregate server-side — never push heavy lifting onto the frontend
alwaysApply: true
---

# Backend does the heavy lifting, not the frontend

Counts, aggregations, joins, and derived/computed fields belong in the backend (SQL or service layer) — not fetched as raw data and computed client-side in a React component or hook. Pushing that work to the frontend makes the UI slower and duplicates logic that the backend can express once, correctly, in the query.

## Do

```ts
// ✅ Backend computes/aliases the derived field once, in the query layer
// (see shapeVisitListRow() deriving encounter_type from visit_type)
return {
  ...visit,
  encounter_type: visit.visit_type ?? visit.encounter_type,
};
```

```ts
// ✅ Backend aggregates counts via SQL, frontend just renders them
availableBeds: sql<number>`(SELECT count(*) FROM beds b WHERE b.unit_id = ${units.id} AND b.status = 'available')`,
```

## Do not

```tsx
// ❌ Fetching all rows and counting/deriving in the frontend
const availableBeds = beds.filter(b => b.status === 'available').length;
const isEmergency = visit.visitType === 'emergency'; // derived client-side because backend didn't already alias it
```

## Applies to

- Any count, sum, or aggregate a list/detail view needs.
- Any field one endpoint computes/aliases (e.g. a list endpoint) that a sibling endpoint (e.g. a single-item detail endpoint) also needs — add it there too, don't leave the frontend to re-derive it from a different raw field.
- Cross-entity summaries and joins.

Exception: genuinely per-viewer/ephemeral UI state (collapsed sections, local filters, draft form values) — that's fine client-side, it isn't domain data.
